---

# Stage 1

Athlete Profile

Purpose

Understand who the athlete is.

Examples

Age

Experience

Combat discipline

Goals

Equipment

Training availability

History

The profile changes slowly.

---

# Stage 2

Current Context

Purpose

Understand today's situation.

Examples

Recovery

Sleep

Stress

Pain

Competition proximity

Previous session

Available time

Travel

Context changes every day.

---

# Stage 3

Recovery Evaluation

Performed by:

Recovery Engine

Outputs

Recovery estimate

Confidence

Recovery constraints

Recovery recommendations

---

# Stage 4

Safety Evaluation

Performed by:

Safety Engine

Outputs

Risk estimation

Contraindications

Exercise limitations

Required modifications

Safety always overrides later decisions.

---

# Stage 5

Periodization Context

Performed by:

Periodization Engine

Outputs

Current phase

Primary adaptation

Secondary adaptations

Long-term priorities

Planning constraints

---

# Stage 6

Training Objective Selection

Performed by:

Decision Engine

Examples

Maximum Strength

Explosive Power

Aerobic Capacity

Technical Conditioning

Recovery Session

Competition Preparation

Only one primary objective should exist.

---

# Stage 7

Capability Prioritization

Performed by:

Capability Selection Engine

Examples

Lower-body power

Upper-body strength

Grip endurance

Rotational power

Anaerobic conditioning

Footwork

Movement quality

Capabilities are prioritized.

Not equally weighted.

---

# Stage 8

Exercise Selection

Performed using:

Exercise Knowledge Base

Selection considers:

Adaptation quality

Equipment

Recovery

Safety

Sport specificity

Progression history

Movement balance

No exercise is selected simply because it is popular.

---

# Stage 9

Session Construction

Performed by:

Session Generation Engine

The engine determines:

Exercise order

Sets

Repetitions

Load

Tempo

Rest

Supersets

Conditioning

Transitions

Warm-up

Cool-down

The session becomes executable.

---

# Stage 10

Progression Assignment

Performed by:

Progression Engine

The engine determines:

Maintain

Increase

Reduce

Modify

Progression applies only after the session structure exists.

---

# Stage 11

Validation

Before delivery,

CAS validates the complete session.

Validation checks include:

Recovery consistency

Safety consistency

Equipment availability

Training duration

Movement balance

Volume

Intensity

Duplicate exercises

Logical transitions

The session must be internally coherent.

---

# Stage 12

Explainability

Performed by:

Explainability Engine

Every important recommendation receives:

Reason

Evidence

Applied principles

Confidence

Alternative possibilities

The athlete understands why.

---

# Stage 13

Delivery

The session is presented.

Examples

Home Screen

Training Screen

Voice Guidance

Apple Watch

Export

Coach Review

The pipeline ends only when the recommendation reaches the athlete.

---

# Feedback Loop

The pipeline continues after the session.

Execution

↓

Observed Results

↓

Adaptation

↓

Learning

↓

Updated Athlete Model

↓

Future Pipeline

Every completed session improves future recommendations.

---

# Failure Handling

The pipeline must never stop because one source of information is missing.

Example

Sleep unavailable

↓

Recovery confidence decreases

↓

Pipeline continues

Graceful degradation is mandatory.

---

# Determinism

Given identical inputs,

CAS should produce identical outputs.

The pipeline should never behave randomly.

Random variation belongs inside explicitly designed variability,

not inside reasoning.

---

# Explainability

Every stage should remain explainable independently.

The athlete may inspect:

Recovery reasoning

↓

Safety reasoning

↓

Progression reasoning

↓

Final recommendation

Transparency is preserved throughout the pipeline.

---

# Human Override

The athlete may modify:

Exercises

Load

Volume

Session timing

Preferences

The pipeline records these modifications.

Future recommendations learn from them.

---

# Learning

Each execution enriches the Athlete Model.

Learning includes:

Recovery behaviour

Adaptation speed

Exercise preferences

Compliance

Pain patterns

Performance response

The pipeline becomes increasingly individualized.

---

# Future Expansion

Future stages may include:

Nutrition Engine

Competition Engine

Psychological Readiness Engine

Environmental Adaptation Engine

Coach Collaboration Engine

The pipeline remains modular.

---

# Success Criteria

The pipeline succeeds when:

Every stage has a clear responsibility.

Every decision is reproducible.

Every recommendation is explainable.

Every recommendation remains scientifically coherent.

Every completed session improves future decisions.

---

# Final Principle

The Generation Pipeline is not a sequence of algorithms.

It is the reasoning process of a professional strength and conditioning coach,

formalized,

modular,

and explainable.

Every recommendation delivered by CAS is the consequence of this reasoning,

never the result of chance.